A||||8||saleenfiero||Clay Bar anyone?||||1||
Z||000000||saleenfiero||08-12-2001||01:05 PM||||Has anyone tried these things?  I don't wanna end up scratching my pain job by using one or anything.  Also, my friend's got a Sunbird with a TON of tree sap on it.  What can get this stuff off?  We've tried strong bug and tar remover and nothing.  Will these bars do anything to help him?||||reg||1
Z||000001||MD_Fiero||08-12-2001||01:20 PM||||i might have a solution for him....friend works at a dealership, and uses this stuff to take off adhesive...tree sap...etc...<P>i will find out prolly later today what its called....let me know if he still needs the name...HTH<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||08-12-2001||01:37 PM||||Clay bar is a mild abrasive. it probably won't help sticky sap and could make it look worse.<P>Try Goo Gone on it. That stuff lifts all kind of sticky crap w/o damaging what's under it.<P>What kind of tree? the type of sap can effect what you'll have to do. High sugar sap like Maple is easier to get off than High Solvent sap like pine. Even when Pine and it's reletives come off, they usually leave plenty of paint damage.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||08-12-2001||04:06 PM||||I've had some success with the clay bar but use the Mother's California Gold NOT the Meguiars (their clay bar sticks and smears)||||reg||1
Z||000004||saleenfiero||08-12-2001||07:26 PM||||I don't have a clue what kind of sap it was.  The car was bought out of town and it looks like the sap has been on there for some time.  I'll look into that goo gone thing, but its not my car so I'll take my time.  Thanx for the replies.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Primaris||08-13-2001||12:01 AM||||I use rubbing alcohol to get pine sap off.  Its not great but works.<P>------------------<BR>Signatures are a waste of bandwidth!||||reg||1
Z||000006||ka4nkf||08-13-2001||12:18 PM||||Try a piece of silk (like a silk scarf) and use it with running water from a hose. This will work wonders on bugs and may work good on sap.||||reg||1
Z||000007||b52buf||08-13-2001||12:55 PM||||You might want to try PEANUT BUTTER. I have used it for years to get pine sap and the like off car. Use the creamy p-butter. It will take some rubbing,keep the area moist with p-butter. After the sap is gone wash off the p-butter with soap and water. Use the rest of the jar for PB&j sandwiches.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Avery Montembeault||08-13-2001||01:03 PM||||I used claybar on my fiero and removed a ****ton of green sap and goo. It was amazing how much it took off, I didnt even realize how much was there! I used the Meguiars, you just gotta make sure you use plenty of the lubricating spray they often sell with it.||||reg||1
